Requirements Gathering Template
As-is capture at L3 activity level
Document purpose	Structured capture of how an activity is performed today, as input to a BRD or automation assessment. This template records the current state only. It does not capture future-state design, solution options, or AI involvement.
Capture unit	One Part A per L2 activity. One Part B per in-scope L3 activity underneath it.
Completed by	
SME(s) interviewed	
Date of capture	
Version	

How to use this template
#	Instruction
1	Fill Part A once for the L2 activity. It carries everything common across the L3s underneath it: intake channels, mailbox addresses, trigger identification, systems, and existing automation.
2	Complete the L3 inventory in Part A before starting any Part B form. That inventory tells you how many Part B forms should exist and which are still outstanding.
3	Fill one Part B per in-scope L3. Part B inherits Part A and records only what is specific to that L3.
4	Where an L3 is materially the same as another already captured, use the "Same as" field at the top of Part B and record only the difference. Do not refill the whole form.
5	Mark shared steps in the as-is table wherever a step also appears in other L3s. These are the candidate reusable components and are easier to identify at capture time than to reverse-engineer later.
6	Capture every numeric threshold at the point it arises, together with the person who owns that number. Thresholds left to a later pass tend to stall as a group because SMEs need to go away and check them.
7	Request real input files at the time of the walkthrough, not afterwards. Sample collection is usually the longest lead item.
8	A worked example for SSG OTC Settlements is at Annexure A. Use it as a reference for the level of detail expected.

Input pattern reference
Guidance: Use these labels consistently. One activity can carry more than one input pattern; tag each channel separately rather than forcing a single label.
Pattern	Description	Typical characteristics
IP1	Request arrives by email containing a link to a webform or system screen	Structured fields plus an unstructured comments box behind the link
IP2	Request arrives by email with a file attachment (Excel or similar)	Attachment may be a controlled template or the requester's own format
IP3	Request arrives as free-text email	No template, no fixed fields, intent has to be read from the body
IP4	Request originates as a workflow item in a system, with no email	Automation starts at receipt of the work item, not at an email
Other	Chat, messaging feed, file drop, phone or any other channel	Describe in full in Part A, Section A3

