Example — Part A, Section A4. Trigger and intent identification
Channel	How the item is recognised as belonging to this activity	Signal reliability H/M/L	What the analyst does when it is ambiguous	Can one item map to multiple L3s? Y/N	If yes: how often, and how is it split today	Handled by one person or routed to several
Ch 1 and 2, shared mailbox	The team lead runs a script that surfaces allege emails from the mailbox. The team lead then allocates them manually, by AM grade and above, against an agreed client allocation list that changes infrequently. The analyst then reads the body and any attachment to establish what the counterparty is asserting: one trade or several, and whether the assertion is a match, an allege or DK, an SSI verification or a reaffirmation.	M	The analyst reads the full body and attachment. Non-operational mail sharing the inbox (out-of-office replies, bounce-backs, counterparty newsletters and KYC requests, duplicate sends, aged mail resurfacing) is identified by reading and set aside.	Y	A single email may concern multiple trades. Parent-and-child structure is seen, where a summary trade sits in the body and the child trade details sit in an attachment, which spans L3 2.1 and 3.1. Separately, a mail may appear counterparty-initiated in the headers while the body references a NEWS-issued thread, which spans Theme 1 and Themes 2 and 3; this is rare.	One analyst handles the whole email. Each trade within it is tracked as a separate cashflow record under one parent email reference.
Ch 3, NEWS thread	NEWS parses the reply automatically where the counterparty has populated the Nomura template correctly. Where it cannot parse the reply, NEWS routes it to "Assigned to User for Investigation" and it reaches the analyst.	H where the template is used correctly	Falls to the analyst as unstructured correspondence.	Y	Where a counterparty drops the NEWS no-reply address from the chain, an otherwise acceptable reply cannot be ingested and lands in Outlook for manual handling.	One analyst.
